[38;5;231m⏺[39m Good — device ID plus account linking. Next fork.

  [1mWhere does the tracked data go?[0m This decides whether the ID needs to be readable by your server, which in turn
  constrains how it's stored — so it comes before the storage question:

  - [1mConsole only, for now[0m — the module owns the IDs and logs events; a real transport gets swapped in later behind the
    same interface. Buys a small, testable change that matches the current stub ([38;5;153mlogin()[39m doesn't call the API yet
    either). Costs nothing now and is cheap to change, as long as the interface is designed for it.
  - [1mPOST to your own backend[0m — events go to an endpoint alongside the existing [38;5;153mAPI_ENDPOINT[39m. Buys data you actually own.
    Costs: needs an endpoint contract, plus failure handling (retry? drop? queue?) since tracking must never break a
    login.
  - [1mThird-party analytics SDK[0m — Segment, PostHog, or similar. Buys dashboards and identity-linking for free ([38;5;153midentify()[39m
    is exactly the device→account link you just chose). Costs a dependency, a vendor, and consent obligations.

  I recommend [1mconsole only for now, behind a real interface[0m. Everything in this repo is a stub, and the durable part of
  this work is the module boundary and the ID lifecycle — not the transport. Picking a backend or a vendor is a decision
  you can make later without reworking call sites, [3mprovided[0m the interface is right, which is what the spec will pin
  down.
